References: <20080402105724.pytpf7o8swocskgw@webmail.utoronto.ca>
Message-ID: <47F645E2.8040507@web.de>

Hi Kate,

You can find extensive code for doing this in the ecological archives 
supplemental files for
Jeremy W. Lichstein, Theodore R. Simons, Susan A. Shriner, and Kathleen 
E. Franzreb. 2002. Spatial autocorrelation and autoregressive models in 
ecology. Ecological Monographs 72:445-463.

at http://www.esapubs.org/archive/mono/M072/007/default.htm

The short answer is that you need to do the randomization for finding 
the reference distribution of Morin's I on the original data and then 
run the model on each randomization and calculate Morin's I for each set 
of the resulting residuals rather than simply running randomizations of 
the residuals of the model on the original data.

HTH

Volker

